From 1148ca6c4b162c2c948bd3462c36f45ef367f6d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pavelgajdo <pavel.gajdo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 22:48:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 22/23] shader: halve the cost of software HMMA on RADV

Ocarina of Time runs neural-network compute passes (HMMA m16n8k16) on
every frame, including its pre-rendered movies. On the Xclipse 960
(RADV, GFX11) they took 115 ms of GPU time per frame, so the title and
cutscenes ran at ~8 fps.

- One loop iteration per K=8 group with its four K pairs unrolled, so
  each pair's source lanes are constants and the rounding needs no
  branch. Unrolling all of K=16 was measured worse: RADV hoists the
  second group's shuffles and the larger networks spill thousands of
  VGPRs.
- A comes from the lane's own quad: a quad broadcast with a constant
  index (a DPP move on AMD) instead of an LDS permute.
- When the shader already has halves, round at each K8 boundary with a
  float->half->float conversion marked NoContraction (RADV fuses it
  into the last FMA) instead of ~10 integer operations.

GPU time per frame on the title movie: 115 ms -> 58 ms; no spills in
any of the hot pipelines. Tests updated for the new shape and a test
added for the conversion path.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 4d3d244657515af71362bb139f8187ab7aa7c6f4)
